  As for the clarifying the concpt of a Dojo, this is in contrast to both the Kauffman Fellowship
  program, as well as the Kauffman Fellowship Special Interest Groups.  Kauffman Fellowship is a
  2yr, structured program, that serves as an Executive MBA for venture capital professionals. The
  Special Interest Groups are one of several ways for "alumni" to stay active and continue to get
  value out of the network of all Kauffman alumni, as well as "survey" attend modules and summits.


  pattern of "guest speaker as presenter or guest speakers as panelists.

  Both the Kauffman Fellows official 2yr program and the "society of alumni" with ways to engage
  such as Special Interest Groups are generally about exposure, knowledge, context, pattern
  recognition, network, and staying "current" on industry trends. This is all contextual knowledge,
  not procedural knowledge.  This is content not skills.  It creates wisdom not craft.

  The Kauffman Fellowship was born and grew up in an age where wisdom and network were almost
  entirely what made VC professionals great.  But we're in a new world now, and we have been for a
  while.  Now there's a lot more data, tools, materials, content, workflows, tons of apps and all
  kinds of procedural skills from "writing excel functions" to "coding your own agent orchestrator
  to generate investment memos"

  The Dojo is my (FullStack VC -- which is not a company, more an initiative and aspiring track for
  Kauffman and VC community) answer to this gap in knowledge, context, widom, pattern recognition,
  and network, into HARD skills of using software and infortmation technology and computing to
  PERFORM tasks and create output.  So, the Dojo is kind of contrasted with but complementary with
  anything the Kauffman Fellowship is doing.  The Dojo is just a short word, catchy marketing, to
  describe a community of practice or a learning community.  There is no program for this, no
  degree, no professors.  The only option is to learn together to thrive in the NEW world that
  requires both soft and hard skills, both human and technical fluency, both relationships and true
  work.